NEO GEO Z80 SOUND DRIVER (YM2610)
=================================

Custom Z80 driver for the YM2610 (OPNB) chip.  Handles all four audio subsystems:
ADPCM-A (6-channel samples), ADPCM-B (delta-T stream), SSG/MML (melody), FM (6 op channels).

COMMUNICATION (68k to Z80)
--------------------------
The 68000 writes a command byte to REG_SOUND ($320000).  The Z80 receives an NMI,
queues the command in a 32-byte FIFO, and processes it on the next timer tick.

  soundInit()               Command $01
                            No arguments. Reinitializes YM2610, timer state, and driver RAM.
  soundReset()              Command $03
                            No arguments. Soft reset of driver state and FIFO.
  soundStopAll()            Command $04
                            No arguments. Stops music, ADPCM-A, ADPCM-B, SSG, and FM.
  soundStopMusic()          Command $0F
                            No arguments. Stops music/SSG only.
  soundCancelFade()         Command $11
                            No arguments. Cancels a running fade and restores stored volumes.
  soundSceneReset()         Calls soundStopAll + 4ms + soundReset + 4ms.
                            No arguments. Use before any major scene transition.
  soundFadeOut()            Command $0A + default speed byte
                            No arguments from C. Uses default fade speed internally.
  soundFadeIn()             Command $12 + default speed byte
                            No arguments from C. Uses default fade speed internally.
  soundFadeOutSpeed(n)      Command $0A + n
                            n = 0..255. Larger values fade out faster.
  soundFadeInSpeed(n)       Command $12 + n
                            n = 0..255. Larger values fade in faster.
  playMusic(n)              Command $20 + n  (MML music track)
                            n = 0..MUSIC_TRACK_COUNT-1.
  playSFX(n)                Command $40 + n  (ADPCM-A sample)
                            n = 0..11 (12 samples).
  playSFXB(n)               Command $80 + n  (ADPCM-B sample)
                            n = 0..3 (4 samples).
  playFMDebug()             Command $30
                            No arguments. Plays FM track 0 through the sequencer.
  playFMTrack(n)            Command $31 + n
                            n = 0..FM_TRACK_COUNT-1 (currently 8). Starts a compiled FM track.
  soundSetFMVolume(n)       Command $13 + n
                            n = 0..15. Sets FM output level.
  playSSGTrack(n)           Command $32 + n
                            n = 0..SSG_TRACK_COUNT-1 (currently 4 source tracks). Starts standalone SSG track.
  soundSetSSGPreset(n)      Command $14 + n (lower nibble)
                            n = 0..15. Applies SSG tone preset from ssg_config.inc.
  playInsertCoinSSG()       Plays SSG track 2 with preset 1. No arguments.
  playVoiceCue(n)           Maps n (1..9) to an ADPCM-A voice sample.
                            1=get ready, 2=oh hey ya, 3=coin, 4=insert coin,
                            5=start, 6=title, 7=intro, 8=attack, 9=samurai.
  playGetReadyVoice()       Plays ADPCM-A sample 10. No arguments.
  playAttackVoice()         Plays ADPCM-A sample 11. No arguments.
  playCoinThenReady()       playInsertCoinSSG + 250ms wait + playGetReadyVoice.
  soundPlayDemoFM(n)        soundSceneReset + soundSetFMVolume(0x0C) + playFMTrack(n).
  soundPlayTitleMusic(n)    soundSceneReset + title gong + ADPCM-B title theme.
                            Current helper ignores n and always uses the title-theme bed.
  soundPlayGameLoop(n)      soundSceneReset + direct ADPCM-B theme select.
                            n=0 stage loop 1, n=1 stage loop 2, n=2 ending/results.
  soundSetTempo(n)          Command $0E + n
                            n = 0..255 raw driver tick divisor.
  soundSetADPCMAVolume(n)   Command $05 + n
                            n = 0..63 recommended.
  soundSetADPCMBVolume(n)   Command $06 + n
                            n = 0..255 recommended.
  soundSetSSGVolume(n)      Command $07 + n
                            n = 0..15 recommended.
  playSoundtest(index)      Raw command byte passthrough for debug.
                            index = 0..255, sent unchanged to the driver.

Argument ranges
---------------
  music track n:  0..MUSIC_TRACK_COUNT-1
  FM track n:     0..FM_TRACK_COUNT-1 (currently 8)
  SSG track n:    0..SSG_TRACK_COUNT-1
  SSG preset n:   0..15
  FM vol n:       0..15
  ADPCM-A n:      0..11 (12 samples)
  ADPCM-B n:      0..3  (4 samples)
  tempo n:        0..255 raw driver tick divisor
  fade speed n:   0..255, higher is faster
  ADPCM-A vol n:  0..63 recommended, written to YM2610 reg $01 on port B
  ADPCM-B vol n:  0..255 recommended, written to YM2610 reg $1B on port A
  SSG vol n:      0..15 recommended, used by the music engine

DRIVER MEMORY MAP
-----------------
  $0000  Reset vector
  $0038  Timer B IRQ (music ticker)
  $0066  NMI handler (command intake)
  $00D0  Driver main code
  $F800  Command FIFO (32 bytes)
  $F820  FIFO read/write pointers
  $F900  YM2610 port-A register shadow
  $FA00  YM2610 port-B register shadow
  $FE00  Driver variables (tempo, volume, channel state, etc.)

YM2610 VOLUME SETTINGS (driver defaults after fixes)
-----------------------------------------------------
  ADPCM-A master level  (reg $01, port B):  $3F = maximum
  ADPCM-A per-channel TL (regs $08-$0D, port B):  $C0 = pan L+R, TL=0 (max)
  ADPCM-B volume        (reg $1B, port A):  $A8 default (override via command)
  SSG volume:           set per-channel via command

FEATURES
--------
  Auto-Stop:     software timers cut ADPCM-A playback to prevent noise tails
  Shadow regs:   avoids redundant YM2610 writes; safe for all register classes
  MML engine:    SSG/FM melody driven by Timer B IRQ at configurable tempo
  ADPCM-B reset: full reset-flagclear-start sequence before each playback
  Fade control:   software fade in/out for music, ADPCM-A, and ADPCM-B volumes

BUILDING
--------
Run `make m1rom` to assemble driver.asm with wla-z80.
Run `make sound` to rebuild MML data, V ROM, and M1 ROM together.
Run `make -f MakefileWin32.mak m1rom` for the native Windows build.
Run `make -f MakefileWin32.mak sound` for the full native Windows sound build.
Run `make m1rom USE_Z80C=1` to compile the experimental C driver while still linking
  the authoritative `driver.asm` into the final playable M1.
Run `make -f MakefileWin32.mak m1rom USE_Z80C=1` for the equivalent native Windows
  comparison build.
Run `make m1rom-c` to link the experimental C runtime path.
  The C runtime uses `driver_prelude.asm` for reset/RST/NMI/IRQ, and `driver.c` starts at `$00D0`.
Run `make -f MakefileWin32.mak m1rom-c` for the equivalent native Windows
  experimental runtime build.
Run `make compare-driver` to compare the ASM and C M1 ROMs.
Run `make samples` to use the bundled Python WAV converter by default.
Set `SOX=/path/to/sox` only when you explicitly want the SoX path.
